oposed conclusion is justified if $f$ is real analytic at $a$?","options":["The Taylor series equals $f$ only at $x = a$, not on any interval around $a$.","There is some $r \\gt 0$ such that $f(x) = \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}f^{(n)}(a)(x-a)^n$ for all $x\\in(a-r,a+r)$.","There is some $r \\gt 0$ such that $f(x) = \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n$ for $x\\in(a-r,a+r)$.","For every $r \\gt 0$, $f(x) = \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n$ for all $x\\in(a-r,a+r)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"536d0f44d06fa836a493bb53","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the Taylor series of $f$ around $a$ built from?","options":["The derivatives $f^{(n)}(x)$ and powers $(x-a)^n$","The derivatives $f^{(n)}(a)$ and powers $x^n$","The first derivative $f'(a)$ and powers $(x-a)^n$","The derivatives $f^{(n)}(a)$ and powers $(x-a)^n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"80f5f1c457e4569055ffab2f","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When is a Taylor series guaranteed to converge to the function near $a$?","options":["When the function is real analytic at $a$","Whenever the function is once differentiable","Whenever the function is bounded","Whenever the function is discontinuous"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2b0b6612f81bbc3484a07b65","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_018","difficulty":"basic","question_ty